# The compression state, as circuit wires

`stateBits t i` is the 32 input nodes carrying working variable `i` after `t` rounds, least
significant bit first. The layout names it directly: `a` after round `t` is `outa (t-1)` and `e`
is `oute (t-1)`, while `b`, `c`, `d` and `f`, `g`, `h` are the three previous values of `a` and
`e`. Before the first round all eight are the incoming chaining words `h0_i`.

Checked against the artifact: for each of the 128 `round_step` assertions the round index is
recovered from its `K` constant (unique per round), and the set of words in its cone matches this
model exactly — 128 of 128, both subclasses.

The payoff is that the shift half of `SHA256.sha256_soundness`'s round hypothesis needs no
per-round work at all: `stateBits_shift` proves it once, for every `t`.
-/

